# Temporary Office lock files (created when an .xlsx is open)
~$*.xlsx

# Images copied out of images/ by scripts/convertData.mjs.
#
# IGNORED because they are byte-identical copies of files already tracked in images/ —
# committing them would double the repository's binary weight (3.3 MB today, more per country)
# to store the same pixels twice. `npm run build` regenerates them, so a clean checkout still
# builds. The originals in images/ remain tracked; only the copies are derived.
#
# NOTE the deliberate asymmetry with src/data/journey.json, which IS committed even though it
# is also generated. A 25 KB text file produces a readable diff — when a number changes, the
# review shows which number and by how much, which is worth having in history for a project
# whose subject is data. A binary copy produces no useful diff at all.
public/images/

# Environment files — never commit secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Claude Code's per-machine settings. `settings.json` (shared, checked in) records permissions the
# whole project should have; `settings.local.json` accumulates one-off approvals full of absolute
# paths from whichever machine granted them, so it stays out of a public repository.
.claude/settings.local.json

# The superseded 12-country workbook. It is kept on disk as the backlog of countries that did not
# make the five-stop itinerary, but it is NOT tracked: its image-URL column still points at an
# internal corporate drive, alias and all, and this repository is public. The workbook the build
# actually reads is travel_data_dictionary_10_countries.xlsx, whose URLs were replaced with local
# filenames — see scripts/validators.mjs, which fails the build if an internal host ever returns.
travel_data_dictionary.xlsx
